/**
\addtogroup optimizers

The `laplace::optimizers` module provides the pieces of unconstrained minimization on the host: the objective concepts (`<laplace/optimizers/objective.hpp>`), the report of a run (`report.hpp`), the stopping rules (`stop_rules.hpp`), two line searches (`line_search.hpp`), the dense curvature solver (`dense_curvature_solver.hpp`) and `minimize()` (`minimize.hpp`), all in the same directory. They are header-only templates that run on the host; none is marked `LAPLACE_HD`. A program that uses `dense_curvature_solver` links `Laplace::dense`. On the standard test problems the line searches accept a step within 13 evaluations along \f$-g\f$ and within one along a Newton direction. Newton's method and the trust region are on the page \ref optimizers_second_order "Newton and trust region"; the quasi-Newton methods BFGS and L-BFGS are on the page \ref optimizers_quasi_newton "Quasi-Newton methods"; the wrapper that runs any optimizer from several starts is on the page \ref optimizers_multistart "Multiple starts"; Brent's method and block alternation are on the page \ref optimizers_block_coordinate "Brent and block alternation"; Nelder-Mead and compass search are on the page \ref optimizers_derivative_free "Nelder-Mead and compass search"; the proximal methods are on the page \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods"; the proximal bundle method is on the page \ref optimizers_bundle "Proximal bundle method"; the primal-dual method for a penalty through a linear map is on the page \ref optimizers_primal_dual "Primal-dual method".

## Objectives

An objective is any type with the members that the concepts require; there is no base class. The point \f$x \in \mathbb{R}^n\f$, without constraints, is a read-only host View, \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point "optimizer_point". Gradients and directions are writable host Views of \f$n\f$ entries, \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector "optimizer_vector", and a symmetric curvature matrix \f$H\f$ is an \f$n \times n\f$ column-major host View, \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix "dense_curvature_matrix".

The objective concepts form a refinement ladder where each member returns \f$f(x)\f$ and evaluates everything requested in one pass, computing derivatives only when asked:
- \ref laplace::optimizers::value_objective "value_objective": requires `double value(x)` returning \f$f(x)\f$.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::gradient_objective "gradient_objective": refines `value_objective` with `double value_gradient(x, g)`, returning \f$f(x)\f$ and writing the gradient (or subgradient) \f$g = \nabla f(x)\f$ into `g`.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_objective "curvature_objective": refines `gradient_objective` with member type `curvature_type` (currently `dense_curvature_matrix`) and `double value_gradient_curvature(x, g, H)`, returning \f$f(x)\f$ and writing \f$g\f$ and the symmetric matrix \f$H\f$.

Whether \f$H\f$ is the observed Hessian or an expected information is the objective's choice. A non-finite value means that the objective cannot be evaluated at \f$x\f$: the line searches refuse such a trial point, without an error.

The \ref laplace::optimizers::composite_objective "composite_objective" concept refines `gradient_objective` for separable sums \f$f = f_{\mathrm{s}} + r\f$. Here `value()` and `value_gradient()` evaluate the smooth term \f$f_{\mathrm{s}}\f$ alone, and \f$r(x) = \sum_b r_b(x_b)\f$ is separable in blocks \f$b\f$. It requires `nonsmooth_value(x)` for the non-smooth term, `nonsmooth_block_count()`, `nonsmooth_prox(v, t, z)` writing \f$z = \operatorname{prox}_{t r}(v) = \arg\min_z (r(z) + \|z - v\|^2 / (2t))\f$ for proximal step \f$t > 0\f$, and `weak_convexity_constant(b)` returning \f$\rho_b \ge 0\f$ such that \f$r_b + \rho_b \|x_b\|^2 / 2\f$ is convex (\f$0\f$ when \f$r_b\f$ is convex). The proximal operator is exact only when \f$t \rho_b < 1\f$. The proximal methods, and the adapter that builds such an objective from penalties, are on the page \ref optimizers_proximal "Proximal methods".

## Report and status

A run returns an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_report "optimizer_report", which says why it stopped, where, and at what cost. The reason is an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_status "optimizer_status". Two values are successes: `converged`, when a stationarity rule held at its tolerance, and `converged_at_resolution`, when the stationarity measure fell below the resolution of the objective, about \f$\varepsilon |f|\f$, so that no smaller decrease can be verified. Here \f$\varepsilon = 2^{-52}\f$ is the machine epsilon of double precision. The other six are `stopped_on_change`, `stalled`, `iteration_budget`, `evaluation_budget`, `line_search_failed` and `start_not_admissible`, each described in its entry. \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_succeeded "optimizer_succeeded()" is true for the two successes only, and \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_factorization "curvature_factorization" reports the outcome of the last factorization of the curvature.

An `optimizer_report` stores the status, the last accepted point with its value and gradient, the decrement, the search size, the outcome of the last factorization, the iterations, the evaluation counts and the elapsed time.The `decrement` is the decrease predicted by the next step in the objective's units: \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top H^{-1} g\f$ when curvature is used, \f$\frac{1}{2} g^\top B g\f$ for a method whose next direction is \f$d = -B g\f$ with inverse-curvature model \f$B\f$, and quiet NaN when neither is available. The `search_size` field holds the final size of the region still searched by a derivative-free method in the units of \f$x\f$, or quiet NaN when none is reported. The `damped_coordinates` field counts coordinate updates whose diagonal curvature was damped by proximal Newton, and is \f$0\f$ for the other methods. Evaluation counts are stored in `value_evaluations`, `gradient_evaluations`, and `curvature_evaluations`. Calling `value_gradient()` adds one to `value_evaluations` and one to `gradient_evaluations`, and `value_gradient_curvature()` adds one to each of the three counters.

An optional \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_trace "optimizer_trace" callback (`std::function<void(const optimizer_trace_point&)>`) receives an \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_trace_point "optimizer_trace_point" once per accepted iteration; the Views it receives are valid during the call only.

## Stopping rules

A stopping rule is any type modeling \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_rule "stop_rule", providing `static constexpr unsigned needs` and `stop_verdict verdict(const stop_rule_input&) const`. The `needs` bitmask combines \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_gradient "stop_needs_gradient", \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_decrement "stop_needs_decrement", \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_prox_mapping "stop_needs_prox_mapping", \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_search_size "stop_needs_search_size", \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_primal_dual "stop_needs_primal_dual", and \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_needs_previous "stop_needs_previous"; the value and the iteration are always available. The concept `stop_rule_for<R, provided>` holds when rule `R` requires no bit outside `provided`, rejecting incompatible rules at compile time.

Verdicts in \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_verdict "stop_verdict" are ordered from weakest to strongest: `none`, `stopped_on_change`, `converged_at_resolution`, and `converged`. Each call receives a \ref laplace::optimizers::stop_rule_input "stop_rule_input" with the quantities of the iteration. Its previous fields are valid only when `has_previous` is `true`, and `gradient` is empty and `decrement` is quiet NaN when not provided by the method; the section "Report and status" above defines the decrement.

The module provides seven rules:
- \ref laplace::optimizers::decrement_stop_rule "decrement_stop_rule": returns `converged` when \f$\delta \le \tau\f$ for predicted decrease \f$\delta\f$ and `tolerance` \f$\tau\f$ (default \f$10^{-6}\f$), `converged_at_resolution` when \f$\delta \le m_{\mathrm{res}} \varepsilon |f|\f$ for `resolution_multiple` \f$m_{\mathrm{res}}\f$ (default \f$1\f$), and `none` otherwise (including when \f$\delta\f$ is NaN).
- \ref laplace::optimizers::gradient_stop_rule "gradient_stop_rule": returns `converged` when \f$\sigma \max_i |g_i| \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} |f|\f$, with defaults `absolute_tolerance` \f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}} = 10^{-6}\f$, `relative_tolerance` \f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}} = 0\f$, and caller-chosen `scale` \f$\sigma = 1\f$ (such as \f$1 / n\f$ for a per-observation gradient).
- \ref laplace::optimizers::search_size_stop_rule "search_size_stop_rule": stationarity rule of the derivative-free methods requiring `stop_needs_search_size`, in the units of \f$x\f$ rather than of \f$f\f$; returns `converged` when `search_size` is at most \f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \max_i |x_i|\f$ (defaults `absolute_tolerance` \f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}} = 10^{-8}\f$ and `relative_tolerance` \f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}} = 0\f$), and `none` otherwise or when NaN.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::prox_mapping_stop_rule "prox_mapping_stop_rule": stationarity rule of the proximal gradient method requiring `stop_needs_prox_mapping`, in the objective's units; returns `converged` when `prox_mapping` is at most `tolerance` \f$\tau\f$ (default \f$10^{-6}\f$), `converged_at_resolution` when at most \f$m_{\mathrm{res}} \varepsilon |f|\f$ (`resolution_multiple`, default \f$1\f$), and `none` otherwise or when NaN.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::primal_dual_stop_rule "primal_dual_stop_rule": stationarity rule of the primal-dual method requiring `stop_needs_primal_dual`, testing the primal-dual measure defined on \ref optimizers_primal_dual "Primal-dual method" in the objective's units; returns `converged` when `primal_dual` is at most `tolerance` \f$\tau\f$ (default \f$10^{-6}\f$), `converged_at_resolution` when at most \f$m_{\mathrm{res}} \varepsilon |f|\f$ (`resolution_multiple`, default \f$1\f$), and `none` otherwise or when NaN.
- \ref laplace::optimizers::objective_change_stop_rule "objective_change_stop_rule": returns `stopped_on_change` when `has_previous` is `true` and \f$|f - f_{\mathrm{prev}}| \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} |f_{\mathrm{prev}}|\f$ (defaults \f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}} = 10^{-12}\f$, \f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}} = 0\f$).
- \ref laplace::optimizers::parameter_change_stop_rule "parameter_change_stop_rule": returns `stopped_on_change` when `has_previous` is `true` and \f$\max_i |x_i - x_{\mathrm{prev}, i}| \le \tau_{\mathrm{abs}} + \tau_{\mathrm{rel}} \max_i |x_{\mathrm{prev}, i}|\f$ (defaults \f$\tau_{\mathrm{abs}} = 10^{-10}\f$, \f$\tau_{\mathrm{rel}} = 0\f$).

Only the stationarity rules, on the decrement, on the gradient, on the proximal gradient mapping, on the primal-dual measure and on the search size, may declare convergence. The combinator \ref laplace::optimizers::any_of "any_of(...)" builds an \ref laplace::optimizers::any_of_stop_rule "any_of_stop_rule" returning the strongest verdict among its rules, or `none` when given zero rules. The combinator \ref laplace::optimizers::all_of "all_of(...)" builds an \ref laplace::optimizers::all_of_stop_rule "all_of_stop_rule" returning the weakest verdict across at least one rule, so combining a convergence rule with a change rule yields `stopped_on_change`. Their `needs` is the union of their rules' needs.

## Line searches

Given a point \f$x\f$, its value \f$f(x)\f$, its gradient \f$g\f$ and a direction \f$d\f$, a line search studies \f$\phi(s) = f(x + s d)\f$ for steps \f$s > 0\f$. The slope \f$\phi'(0) = g^\top d\f$ must be negative and finite, or the search fails at once with `not_descent_direction`. Sufficient decrease requires \f$\phi(s) - \phi(0) \le c_1 s \phi'(0)\f$, and strong curvature requires \f$|\phi'(s)| \le c_2 |\phi'(0)|\f$. Sufficient decrease is evaluated as the difference \f$\phi(s) - \phi(0)\f$ because the sum \f$\phi(0) + c_1 s \phi'(0)\f$ rounds to \f$\phi(0)\f$ once \f$c_1 s |\phi'(0)|\f$ falls below the rounding unit of \f$\phi(0)\f$ and would accept a step that decreases nothing.

The \ref laplace::optimizers::armijo_line_search "armijo_line_search" struct backtracks by safeguarded interpolation (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996, Algorithm A6.3.1), using value evaluations only. Its default settings are `sufficient_decrease` \f$c_1 = 10^{-4}\f$, `min_shrink` \f$0.1\f$, `max_shrink` \f$0.5\f$, `max_evaluations` \f$60\f$, and `resolution` \f$0\f$. On the first backtrack it computes the minimizer of the quadratic through \f$\phi(0)\f$, \f$\phi'(0)\f$, and \f$\phi(s)\f$, and on subsequent backtracks the minimizer of the cubic through \f$\phi(0)\f$, \f$\phi'(0)\f$, and the last two trial values, kept in \f$[0.1 s,\, 0.5 s]\f$ with the default bounds. On acceptance, `x_trial` holds \f$x + s d\f$.

The \ref laplace::optimizers::wolfe_line_search "wolfe_line_search" struct enforces both sufficient decrease and strong curvature by bracketing and zoom (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithms 3.5 and 3.6, eq. 3.59; Moré and Thuente, 1994). Its default settings are `sufficient_decrease` \f$c_1 = 10^{-4}\f$, `curvature` \f$c_2 = 0.9\f$, `max_evaluations` \f$30\f$, and `resolution` \f$0\f$. Let \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ denote the bracket endpoint satisfying sufficient decrease with the lowest objective value seen, and \f$s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ the opposite bracket endpoint. Bracketing extrapolates using the cubic minimizer clamped to \f$[s + 1.1(s - s_{\mathrm{lo}}),\, s + 4(s - s_{\mathrm{lo}})]\f$. Inside zoom, the next trial is the cubic minimizer unless \f$\phi(s_{\mathrm{hi}}) > \phi(s_{\mathrm{lo}})\f$. When the value rose at \f$s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$, zoom selects the cubic minimizer if it is closer to \f$s_{\mathrm{lo}}\f$ than the minimizer of the quadratic through \f$\phi(s_{\mathrm{lo}})\f$, \f$\phi'(s_{\mathrm{lo}})\f$, and \f$\phi(s_{\mathrm{hi}})\f$, and otherwise their mean. Each zoom trial stays a tenth of the bracket from either end. Every trial evaluates both value and gradient; on acceptance, `x_trial` and `g_trial` hold the accepted point and its gradient and `slope` holds \f$\phi'(s)\f$.

Both searches treat a non-finite trial value (or non-finite slope in Wolfe) as a refused point counted in `refused_evaluations`. Armijo multiplies the step by `max_shrink` without interpolating through the refused point. Wolfe halves the step toward the last finite trial during bracketing, or replaces \f$s_{\mathrm{hi}}\f$ with the refused step and bisects during zoom. When `resolution` \f$> 0\f$, the search checks whether \f$s_0 |\phi'(0)|\f$ at the initial step \f$s_0\f$ is strictly less than `resolution` before any evaluation and fails with `below_resolution` (never inside backtracking). A trial point equal to `x` in every coordinate in either search, or a Wolfe bracket narrower than \f$4\varepsilon\f$ times the larger endpoint, fails with `step_below_rounding` and is never accepted. The budget `max_evaluations` ends a search with `evaluation_budget`. Both searches return a \ref laplace::optimizers::line_search_result "line_search_result", whose `outcome` is a \ref laplace::optimizers::line_search_outcome "line_search_outcome".

The table gives the evaluations measured from the standard starting points with initial step \f$1\f$. The quadratics have \f$n = 10\f$ and condition \f$\kappa_2\f$, the ratio of the largest to the smallest eigenvalue of their Hessian. The last row uses the Newton direction \f$-H(x_0)^{-1} g\f$ on every problem whose \f$H(x_0)\f$ is positive definite. See `docs/numerics.md`.

| Problem | Direction | Armijo evaluations | Wolfe evaluations |
|---|---|---|---|
| Rosenbrock (\f$n = 2\f$ and \f$100\f$) | \f$-g\f$ | 5 | 5 |
| Powell singular | \f$-g\f$ | 6 | 4 |
| Beale | \f$-g\f$ | 3 | 3 |
| Brown badly scaled | \f$-g\f$ | 2 | 2 |
| Helical valley | \f$-g\f$ | 4 | 4 |
| Wood | \f$-g\f$ | 10 | 6 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^2\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 3 | 3 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^6\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 7 | 7 |
| Quadratic, \f$\kappa_2 = 10^{12}\f$ | \f$-g\f$ | 13 | 13 |
| Positive-definite start | \f$-H(x_0)^{-1} g\f$ | 1 | 1 |

## Dense curvature solver

The \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_solver "dense_curvature_solver" class applies the Cholesky routines of \ref dense to matrices of a fixed size `n`. Calling `factorize(H)` or `factorize_shifted(H, lambda)` reads only the lower triangle of `H` without modifying `H`, computes the Cholesky factor of \f$H\f$ or \f$H + \lambda I\f$, and returns a `curvature_factorization` code (also returned by `last_factorization()`). Incompatible matrix dimensions throw `std::invalid_argument`.

After a `positive_definite` factorization, `newton_direction(g, d)` computes \f$d = -(H + \lambda I)^{-1} g\f$ and returns `true`; if the last factorization did not succeed or the solve fails, it leaves `d` untouched and returns `false`. Calling `condition_estimate()` returns the \ref dense 1-norm condition estimate \f$\kappa_1\f$ of the last factorized matrix, or \f$+\infty\f$ when `last_factorization()` is not `positive_definite`. The trait `curvature_solver_for<H>::type` maps a curvature matrix type to its solver (`dense_curvature_solver` for `dense_curvature_matrix`). The test checks both solves with a relative tolerance of \f$10^{-14}\f$, and the outcomes on an indefinite and on a non-finite matrix.

## Running an optimizer

The concept \ref laplace::optimizers::optimizer_for "optimizer_for\<Optimizer, Objective\>" holds when `optimizer.run(objective, x0)` returns an `optimizer_report`. The free function \ref laplace::optimizers::minimize "minimize(optimizer, objective, x0)" forwards to `optimizer.run(objective, x0)`. A maximum of \f$f\f$ is found by minimizing \f$-f\f$.

## Example

The example evaluates the value and the gradient of \f$f(x) = \frac{1}{2} \|x\|^2\f$ in two variables and runs an Armijo line search along \f$-g\f$, on the host, after `Kokkos::initialize`.

```cpp
#include <laplace/optimizers/line_search.hpp>

struct quadratic_objective {
  double value(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x) const {
    return 0.5 * (x(0) * x(0) + x(1) * x(1));
  }
  double value_gradient(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x,
                        const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector& g) const {
    g(0) = x(0);
    g(1) = x(1);
    return value(x);
  }
};

bool armijo_example() {
  constexpr int n = 2;
  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector x("x", n), g("g", n), d("d", n), x_trial("x_trial", n);
  x(0) = 3.0;
  x(1) = -4.0;
  quadratic_objective objective;
  const double fx = objective.value_gradient(x, g);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) d(i) = -g(i);
  const laplace::optimizers::armijo_line_search search;
  const laplace::optimizers::line_search_result r = search.search(objective, x, fx, g, d, 1.0, x_trial);
  return r.accepted();  // step 1, value 0: the minimizer
}
```

